  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Sewer networks are essential for public health and environmental protection in urban areas, but they are deteriorating due to age, poor maintenance, and population growth. These deteriorations require repair or rehabilitation, but limited budgets force utility managers to prioritize projects. Thus, effective prioritization of sewer rehabilitation is crucial for optimizing resource allocation. This study proposes an integrated decision support system combining Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) and Geographic Information Systems (GIS) to prioritize sewer system rehabilitation in Gdyel, Algeria based on both physical and hydraulic criteria. A set of 18 indicators was used to rank the sewer network in Gdyel Municipality, under two different scenarios. The results showed that 13% of the network in Scenario 1 and 31% in Scenario 2 require urgent intervention. To assess the spatial distribution of the prioritization results in relation to the available reference information, the model-generated risk maps were compared with high-, moderate-, and low-risk zones delineated based on information from the Oran Water and Sanitation Company (SEOR) diagnostic study. The diagnostic point observations were interpolated using Inverse Distance Weighted (IDW) in ArcGIS to produce continuous risk surfaces. The approach showed spatial correspondence between the high-priority areas and the identified high-risk zones, supporting its potential for rehabilitation planning. It can support engineers and sanitation officers in decision-making for more informed and targeted interventions, and can be adapted and applied to other sewer networks worldwide.",
